Mandates utilizing specific aerial photograph scales matching the type and stage of river valley investigation:
a) Reconnaissance/Regional Studies: 1 : 50 000 to 1 : 60 000;
b) Feasibility/Design Studies: 1 : 25 000 to 1 : 30 000;
c) Detailed Site Investigations (Dams, Powerhouses, Tunnels): 1 : 10 000 to 1 : 15 000;
d) Micro-Detail / Foundation Exploration: 1 : 5 000.

Enforces a strict, orderly workflow of four initial photogeological operations:
1) Laying out loose photographic mosaics and checking overlaps;
2) Perusal of the entire area under the stereoscope and noting preliminary geology and morphology;
3) Locating the approximate centre of overlap between adjoining photographs; and
4) Transferring points and inking them in on the working sheets.

To prevent under-seepage and preserve foundation stability:
a) River-side borrow pits must be located at least 3 m from the toe of the embankment;
b) Country-side borrow pits must be located at least 3 m (with a longitudinal drain and 0.6 m minimum cover) or 6 m from the toe.

Specifies recommended modular discharge coefficients (CDe), head correction factors (kh), and standard uncertainties based on three standard crest cross-slopes (as shown in Table 4):
a) Slope 1:40 or less: CDe = 0.630 (or 0.625), kh = 0.0004 m (0.4 mm), Uncertainty = 1.25%;
b) Slope 1:20: CDe = 0.625 (or 0.620), kh = 0.0005 m (0.5 mm), Uncertainty = 1.4%;
c) Slope 1:10: CDe = 0.620 (or 0.615), kh = 0.0008 m (0.8 mm), Uncertainty = 1.15%.

Mandates training and careful measurement to minimize errors, enforcing five specific precautions:
a) Calipers and other measuring instruments must be properly maintained and accurate;
b) Operators must precisely locate the correct point of measurement (PoM) on the tree;
c) The plane of the calipers must be kept at right angles (perpendicular) to the longitudinal axis of the tree;
d) Correct mechanical pressure must be applied at the moment of measurement; and
e) The caliper's bar must be pressed firmly against the tree bole to minimize sliding arm maladjustment errors.

The benchmarking system must evaluate participating partners across six mandatory performance categories:
a) Reliability;
b) Quality;
c) Resources;
d) Customer service;
e) Sustainability; and
f) Personnel and social development.

The finalized BI is categorized and color-coded into four operational performance bands:
a) BI <= 0.75: Poor (Assessment Color Coding: Red);
b) 0.76 < BI <= 1.50: Satisfactory;
c) 1.51 < BI <= 2.25: Good;
d) 2.26 < BI <= 3.00: Excellent.

Conserved seeds must be stored under dry, hermetic conditions using locally available and cost-effective resources:
a) Traditional containers such as earthen pots or bamboo baskets may be used if protective measures against moisture, pests, and rodents are provided;
b) Exposure to high humidity, heat, and direct sunlight must be strictly avoided;
c) For bulk storage, hermetically sealed moisture-impervious bins must NOT be used, as they cause heat and moisture accumulation in lower layers.
